How a restore in place writes a head: `auto` restores into a stage of the
heads whose storage makes one, a zfs dataset, as a clone of it, or a
directory, as a sibling of it, and switches the head to its stage once
every head of the run is restored, at once, so the head is at any time what
the last restore written whole left; the other heads are restored in place,
emptied then written, and marked incomplete meanwhile, which a start
refuses to run on. `always` refuses a restore of a head with no stage,
before anything is written. `never` restores every head in place.

The `--stage` option of a restore takes over.
